{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# APPROVED BY THE OPERATOR — implement Option 3 (scheduled main-health CI, every 6 hours)\n\nThe research below is complete and the recommendation has been **approved**. Do not re-litigate\nthe approach; the operator's instruction and the constraints are recorded in\n\"Operator's decision\" immediately below, and the acceptance criteria at the end are binding.\n\n## Operator's decision (the thing to implement)\n\n1. **GitHub CI for `main` runs on a SCHEDULE, not on push to `main`.** Cadence: **every 6 hours**\n   — that is the signal density the operator wants. Use an **off-the-hour minute** (not `0`) so\n   this repo's runs do not pile onto the hour boundary with every other project on the platform.\n2. **Evaluate EVERY GitHub Actions workflow so the policy is CONSISTENT** — no workflow keeps a\n   push-on-`main` trigger merely because nobody looked at it. Each workflow gets an explicit,\n   recorded disposition.\n3. **External-integration jobs stay at MOST once a day.** Their frequency must not increase.\n   `external-integration.yml` is weekly today and stays weekly.\n4. **Gerrit-verify and the reconciler workflows MUST NOT be changed** — they are separate\n   mechanisms with their own semantics (`gerrit-verify.yaml`'s concurrency keys on\n   `GERRIT_CHANGE_ID` as a deliberate stale-patchset guard; the reconcilers deliberately run\n   with `cancel-in-progress: false`).\n5. **`workflow_dispatch` must remain (or be added)** on every main-health workflow, so anyone can\n   ask \"is `main` healthy?\" on demand without waiting for the tick.\n6. **Pay for the loss of per-commit attribution, explicitly.** Two items from the approved\n   recommendation are NOT optional:\n   - Write the **`git bisect run` command into the workflow's FAILURE OUTPUT**, so remediation is\n     one copy-paste. A scheduled run covers many commits; the first responder must not have to\n     reconstruct the technique under pressure.\n   - **Publish the last-known-green `main` SHA** so a bisect has a lower bound (Zuul's periodic\n     pipelines and vLLM's \"greenest commit on main\" practice are the precedent).\n\n## The confirmed mechanism (verify, do not re-derive)\n\n`test.yml` declares:\n\n```yaml\nconcurrency:\n  group: test-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\n```\n\nOn `main`, `github.ref` is the constant `refs/heads/main`, so every push to `main` lands in ONE\nconcurrency group and cancels the previous run. Cancelled runs render as a red X on the commit\nlist, and GitHub offers no native way to suppress that (see mechanics below).\n\nNote that the affected workflows filter pushes with `branches-ignore: [\"feature/**\"]`, which\n**includes `main`** — so `main` must be added to that ignore list explicitly. Branch-push CI for\nNON-`main` branches is preserved.\n\n## The problem (original framing)\n\nGerrit is the source of truth; GitHub is a read-only mirror. Gerrit's `Verified` label (CI per\npatchset) is the real merge gate. GitHub Actions ALSO runs on `main` post-merge as a safety net.\n\nWhen commits land close together the mirror's `main` runs get CANCELLED as superseded, so\nGitHub shows a long trail of red X's on a `main` that is actually healthy.\n\nOperator's framing: do NOT spend cycles validating every individual commit — Gerrit's\nper-patchset gate is the protection. Main CI matters as a safety net, but the value is\n\"is main healthy NOW\", not \"was every commit green\". Mirror what comparable projects do.\n\n## What comparable projects actually do (VERIFIED via GitHub API)\n\n| Mirror repo | Workflows | Check runs on latest main commit |\n|---|---|---|\n| golang/go | NONE (.github/workflows 404) | **0** |\n| openstack/nova | NONE | **0** |\n| LibreOffice/core | lockdown.yml only (issue-locking bot) | **0** |\n| aosp-mirror/platform_frameworks_base | - | **0** |\n| qt/qtbase | ninja-build.yml, `on: push` | outlier - see below |\n\n**Four of five major Gerrit-primary projects surface NO per-commit CI on the GitHub mirror at\nall.** The mirror is a code mirror, full stop. Qt is the exception and its answer is to make the\njob cheap enough that cancellation never matters (\"finish as fast as possible\", system libs,\nccache) rather than to report per-commit truth.\n\nWhere their real post-merge signal lives:\n- **Go** - post-submit on LUCI (ci.chromium.org/p/golang), dashboard at build.golang.org. Never GitHub.\n- **OpenStack/Zuul** - the closest precedent to what the operator wants, and it is a FIRST-CLASS\n  FEATURE, not a workaround. Zuul's `post` pipeline uses the **supercedent** manager, which\n  collapses queued items per project-ref: \"If more than one additional item is enqueued for the\n  project-ref, previously enqueued items which have not started processing are removed.\" That is\n  literally \"test main's current state, not every commit\". Separate `periodic` (daily 02:00 UTC),\n  `periodic-weekly`, `periodic-stable` pipelines carry the health-over-time signal.\n- **Kubernetes Prow/Tide** - batches PRs, tests the batch, merges together. Tide's docs describe\n  NO post-merge branch testing; the model is entirely pre-merge.\n- **Chromium/ChromeOS** - post-submit builders trigger every **7-10 hours**, deliberately covering\n  many commits at once, with sheriffs watching. This is the direct answer to the bisect question.\n\n## GitHub Actions mechanics (VERIFIED against docs)\n\n- **Cancelled == red X cannot be fixed natively.** GitHub staff acknowledged it Dec 2021; still\n  open in 2026 (community discussion 8336).\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` alone does NOT fix it.** With the default `queue: single`, an\n  existing PENDING run in the group is cancelled and replaced - you still get a red X, just on a\n  different run.\n- **A workflow cannot decline to report.** Actions creates CHECK RUNS intrinsically; the\n  `statuses:` permission governs only commit statuses pushed via API, so `statuses: none` does\n  nothing.\n- **The only \"hide\" path is deleting runs after the fact** (MercuryTechnologies/delete-cancelled-runs).\n- **GitHub merge queue is structurally inapplicable** - `merge_group` is driven by PRs entering a\n  queue; with Gerrit owning the merge decision there are no PRs and `main` advances by mirror push.\n\n## Options (option 3 was chosen)\n\n1. **Drop mirror CI entirely** (Go / OpenStack / LibreOffice / AOSP model). Zero red X's, zero\n   cycles. Cost: loses the safety net unless a Gerrit-side post-merge job replaces it.\n2. **Collapse instead of cancel** (Zuul supercedent semantics). GitHub cannot express this\n   natively; closest is today's behavior plus run deletion. Hack by its own author's admission.\n3. **Schedule-only main health run** (`on: schedule`, drop the `push` trigger for `main`).\n   The ONLY option that removes cancellations by construction rather than hiding them. Surfaces\n   as a workflow badge / Actions tab, not a per-commit mark - which matches the stated intent.\n   Cost: attribution.  **← CHOSEN, at a 6-hour cadence.**\n4. **Keep per-commit but make it cheap** (Qt model). Preserves attribution; costs cycles\n   proportional to commit rate.\n\n**The attribution cost, and how these projects pay it:** Chromium proves batching is survivable -\npost-submit every 7-10 hours, accepting a multi-commit regression range, closed by AUTOMATED\nbisection (Findit: median 14 min for compile failures, 24 min for swarmed gtest) plus a HUMAN on\nsheriff rotation plus tree-closure to stop the bleeding. OpenStack pays it differently: the gate\npipeline is strong enough that post-merge breakage is rare, and `post` jobs are largely artifact\npromotion rather than validation.\n\nBecause Gerrit's `Verified` gate already tests every patchset, a red scheduled run means one of:\na semantic conflict between two independently-verified changes, environmental/dependency drift,\nor a flake. The commit window between last-green and red is small enough that\n`git bisect run <the same CI script>` resolves it in log2(n) steps - typically 3-5 builds.\n\n## What NOT to do, and why\n\n- **delete-cancelled-runs**: makes the timeline LOOK right while destroying the record, and fails\n  open - a genuine failure racing a delete is indistinguishable.\n- **`cancel-in-progress: false` as a one-liner**: does not work (see mechanics above).\n- **GitHub merge queue**: structurally inapplicable.\n- **`queue: max`**: does produce all-green per-commit, but spends MORE cycles than today and\n  directly contradicts the stated intent.\n\n## Scope — the trigger inventory this change reasons over\n\nCandidates (run on push to `main` today):\n\n| Workflow | Trigger today | Cancels? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `test.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | yes (`test-${{ github.ref }}`) |\n| `optionality.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | no |\n| `verify-identity.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch | no |\n| `prompt-eval.yml` | push `branches-ignore: [feature/**]` + PR + dispatch + weekly | yes |\n| `codeql.yml` | push `branches: [main]` + weekly | yes |\n| `terraform-drift.yml` | push `branches: [main]` path-filtered `infra/terraform/**` + daily + dispatch | no |\n\nOut of scope, MUST NOT be modified: `gerrit-verify.yaml`, `reconcile-bridge.yml`,\n`reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`, `external-integration.yml`, `mirror-guard.yml`, `lockdown.yml`,\n`release.yml`, `jira-dc-*.yml`, `structured-output-baseline.yml`, and the four `workflow_call`\nreusables (`_artifact-probe.yml`, `_build-and-test.yml`, `_eval-discipline.yml`,\n`_optionality.yml`) — the reusables have no triggers of their own, so only their CALLERS change.\n\n## The safety property that makes this acceptable\n\nPre-merge gating is **unchanged**. The Gerrit `Verified` lane runs the reusable\n`_build-and-test.yml` (and `_artifact-probe.yml`) through `gerrit-verify.yaml`'s\n`workflow_dispatch`, which is triggered by Gerrit per patchset. Editing `test.yml`'s **triggers**\ncannot alter that lane, because the lane never reads `test.yml`. Every change still needs\n`LLM-Review +1` AND `Verified +1` to land. The mirror's `main` CI is, and remains, a safety net —\nthe change only alters WHEN the safety net runs, never what gates a merge.\n\n## Files this touches\n\n`.github/workflows/test.yml`, `.github/workflows/optionality.yml`,\n`.github/workflows/verify-identity.yml`, `.github/workflows/prompt-eval.yml`,\n`.github/workflows/codeql.yml`, `.github/workflows/terraform-drift.yml`, a new\n`scripts/main_health_report.py` (the report renderer, kept out of the workflow YAML so the\nsuite can drive it), and two new test modules under `tests/unit/`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] **AC1 — No workflow triggers on a push to `refs/heads/main`.** For each of `test.yml`,\n      `optionality.yml`, `verify-identity.yml`, `prompt-eval.yml`, `codeql.yml`, and\n      `terraform-drift.yml`, a push event whose ref is `refs/heads/main` matches no trigger.\n- [ ] **AC2 — The four `branches-ignore` workflows exclude `main` explicitly.** `test.yml`,\n      `optionality.yml`, `verify-identity.yml`, and `prompt-eval.yml` list `main` alongside\n      `feature/**` in `push.branches-ignore`.\n- [ ] **AC3 — The two `branches: [main]` workflows drop their `push` key outright.**\n      `codeql.yml` and `terraform-drift.yml` have no `push` trigger at all (each had only\n      `branches: [main]`, so there is no other branch to preserve).\n- [ ] **AC4 — Push CI for NON-`main` branches is preserved.** The four `branches-ignore`\n      workflows still trigger on a push to a topic branch such as `wip-some-topic`.\n- [ ] **AC5 — `feature/**` stays excluded.** The existing S3-replicated feature-branch\n      exclusion is unchanged in all four `branches-ignore` workflows.\n- [ ] **AC6 — The three cheap branch-health lanes gain a 6-hourly schedule.** `test.yml` gets\n      `41 */6 * * *`, `optionality.yml` gets `47 */6 * * *`, `verify-identity.yml` gets\n      `53 */6 * * *` — four \"is main healthy\" runs a day each, at deliberately staggered\n      off-the-hour minutes so this repo's runs neither pile onto the hour boundary that every\n      other project schedules onto nor onto each other.\n- [ ] **AC7 — `prompt-eval.yml` keeps its weekly cron `0 7 * * 1` and gains no 6-hourly cron.**\n      Its live tier is billable; 6-hourly would be a 28x cost increase nobody asked for.\n- [ ] **AC8 — `codeql.yml` keeps its weekly cron `23 4 * * 1` and gains no 6-hourly cron.** A\n      security scanner's cadence is a deliberate cost/coverage choice.\n- [ ] **AC9 — `terraform-drift.yml` keeps its daily cron `17 6 * * *` and gains no 6-hourly\n      cron.** The daily sweep is already this workflow's branch-health signal.\n- [ ] **AC10 — Every one of the six workflows has `workflow_dispatch`**, so on-demand \"is main\n      healthy?\" works without waiting for the tick (`codeql.yml` gains one; it had none).\n- [ ] **AC11 — The last-known-green `main` SHA is resolved from the workflow's own successful\n      run history** via the Actions API (`status=success` on the current branch). No new state\n      is persisted: the GitHub side is a read-only mirror, so nothing is pushed.\n- [ ] **AC12 — A GREEN scheduled/dispatched run publishes its own SHA as the new last-known-green\n      value** to `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`, so the lower bound is discoverable before an incident,\n      not only during one.\n- [ ] **AC13 — A RED scheduled/dispatched run publishes a copy-pasteable remediation recipe** to\n      `$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY`: a `git bisect start <bad> <last-green>` line with both SHAs filled\n      in, followed by a `git bisect run` command using this repo's own CI reproduction\n      (`uv sync --locked --extra dev`, then `make check`, then `make test`).\n- [ ] **AC14 — Unresolvable last-known-green degrades gracefully.** Whenever the lower bound\n      cannot be established — the Actions API returns no earlier successful run, OR the call\n      itself fails (timeout, 4xx/5xx, rate limit, malformed body) — the report emits a recipe\n      with a VISIBLE placeholder lower bound plus an explanation, and the step still exits 0. A\n      missing lower bound must never turn a green `main` red, and a plausible-but-wrong SHA\n      would send the bisect through commits that were never green.\n- [ ] **AC15 — The report is emitted from a NEW caller-level job in `test.yml`, never from a\n      reusable.** `test.yml`'s gates run inside `_build-and-test.yml` / `_artifact-probe.yml`,\n      which are in the untouchable set and accept no caller steps; the report therefore lives in\n      a caller-level `main-health-report` job with `if: always()` that `needs` every gating job.\n      It is carried by `test.yml` only — that is the lane whose failure implies a code\n      regression worth bisecting; `optionality.yml` and `verify-identity.yml` are single-purpose\n      checks whose failure names its own cause.\n- [ ] **AC16 — The report's rendering logic is a repo script, not inline YAML**, so it is\n      unit-testable off a CI runner (workflow YAML is not reachable by the test suite).\n- [ ] **AC17 — The live external tier does not fire more often.** Dispatching `test.yml` is now\n      the routine \"is main healthy?\" button, so its `external` job requires an explicit\n      `run_external` dispatch input defaulting to false, and the schedule can never reach it.\n- [ ] **AC18 — `external-integration.yml` is unmodified** and stays on its weekly\n      `0 6 * * 1` cron, within the once-a-day cap.\n- [ ] **AC19 — The separate mechanisms are byte-identical.** `git diff` shows no change to\n      `gerrit-verify.yaml`, `reconcile-bridge.yml`, `reconcile-bridge-canary.yml`,\n      `mirror-guard.yml`, or any `_*.yml` reusable.\n- [ ] **AC20 — Automated regression coverage pins the trigger policy** — a test asserts, per\n      workflow, that a push to `refs/heads/main` does not match, that non-`main` push CI\n      survives, that each cadence is as specified, that dispatch is present, and that the\n      untouchable set is unchanged.\n- [ ] **AC21 — Automated coverage pins the report rendering** — tests assert the green path\n      names the head SHA as the new lower bound, the red path emits a `git bisect start` line\n      carrying both the bad and the resolved good SHA plus a `git bisect run` command, and the\n      empty-history path emits the degraded message instead of raising.\n- [ ] **AC22 — Every touched workflow parses and lints.** `make lint` (which runs actionlint\n      over every workflow), `make typecheck`, and `make test` are all green.\n- [ ] **AC23 — The Verified-gate parity guard stays green.**\n      `scripts/check_verify_gate_parity.py` fails the build when a job that gates `main` on a\n      mirror lane is absent from `gerrit-verify.yaml`'s `vote.needs`. The new\n      `main-health-report` job is schedule/dispatch-only and never runs on the push/PR critical\n      path, so it is added to that script's `EXCLUDED_JOBS` with a one-line justification —\n      the documented escape hatch, alongside `external` and `eval-live`. `gerrit-verify.yaml`\n      itself is NOT edited, so AC19 holds.\n- [ ] **AC24 — The policy is written down outside this ticket.** `CONTRIBUTING.md` §3 (\"GitHub\n      is a read-only mirror\") gains a short subsection stating that mirror `main` CI is\n      scheduled, why (cancelled runs render as red X on a healthy `main`), that pre-merge\n      gating is unaffected, and how to respond to a red tick (dispatch to re-check; the run\n      summary carries the bisect recipe and the last-known-green SHA).\n\n## Verification\n\n- `make lint` — includes actionlint over every workflow file (AC22).\n- `make typecheck` and `make test` (AC22).\n- `tests/unit/test_ci_main_push_policy.py` — the trigger-policy suite: AC1-AC10, AC15, AC17,\n  AC18, AC19, AC20.\n- `tests/unit/test_main_health_report.py` — the report-rendering suite driving the script\n  directly with stubbed inputs: AC12, AC13, AC14, AC21.\n- Recorded in the change itself: for each changed workflow, the resulting `on:` block and the\n  explicit reason `refs/heads/main` no longer matches it (AC1).\n\n## Explicitly NOT determined (do not treat as settled)\n\n- Whether qt/qtbase sets a `concurrency` block - its all-green story is INFERRED from the trigger\n  and speed comments, not confirmed.\n- Whether OpenStack/Wikimedia `post` pipelines run VALIDATION or only artifact/doc publishing.\n- Whether AOSP/Chromium mirrors deliberately suppress checks or simply have no workflows - the\n  observable (0 check runs) was verified, the intent was not.\n- Whether Findit still runs today and whether it auto-reverts.\n- Kubernetes post-merge: verified only that Tide's docs do not cover it.\n- GA status of the `queue:` concurrency option on the free/public Actions tier.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785946028731758001,"uuid":"ee5ac2ef-6d7d-4d7a-b8b9-0c22fa9aa06a"}